// Pipelined schedule: producer and consumer stages on different
// workers, async/buffered streaming between them.
//
// Three workers:
//   host      -- runs the effectful I/O kernels (load_input,
//                save_output). Owns the file system.
//   producer  -- runs `produce` (stage 1).
//   consumer  -- runs `transform` (stage 2).
//
// The load-bearing cross-worker edge is `stream` (producer -> consumer);
// it is the only data symbol the pipelined `for n` loop produces on
// one worker and consumes on another. `seeds` and `result` are
// fan-in/fan-out edges that touch the host outside the pipelined loop.
//
// Capability surface exercised (PRD §9 row 9, M4):
//   - `transfer stream : async, buffer=4, notify=event`
//     The async-streaming channel. Buffer=4 = pipeline depth so a
//     fast producer doesn't block the consumer at steady state.
//     notify=event = condvar-style readiness notification (not a
//     barrier-style sync), which only the async backend supports.
//   - `loop n : pipeline=4`
//     Sample n on the consumer side computing `transform` while
//     sample n+1 is on the producer side computing `produce`.
//
// Why three workers, not two
// --------------------------
// The task spec says "two workers (producer, consumer); place produce
// on producer; place transform on consumer". Placing `produce` on
// `producer` and `transform` on `consumer` exercises the cross-worker
// `stream` edge. The host is implicit in the schedule the same way it
// is in 02-split-add/split and 13-cnn-inference/pipeline_parallel:
// effectful I/O kernels need somewhere to live, and the host is the
// only worker that owns the file system. Calling out the three
// workers explicitly in `workers = { ... }` is the project convention
// (see 13/pipeline_parallel.sched.nuc).
//
// Why this schedule cannot run on pthreads-sync / mp-tcp-bufsync
// -------------------------------------------------------------
// Both backends' capabilities.toml declare `supports_async = false`,
// `supports_buffer = false`, `notify = ["polled"|"barrier"]`. The
// `transfer stream` clause here requires all three (async + buffer>1 +
// notify=event), so the capability check rejects this schedule on
// those backends BEFORE codegen. The e2e harness reports the
// corresponding cells as [[skip]] with the cited capability mismatch;
// see e2e-matrix.toml. pthreads-async (TASK-0228 / TASK-0229) is the
// tier-1 backend whose capability surface satisfies this schedule.
//
// What's intentionally NOT here
// -----------------------------
//   - No `block=`, no `vectorize=`. The pipeline transform is the
//     only loop transform on `n`.
//   - No distributed placement (`place produce on { p0, p1, ... }`).
//     A single producer and a single consumer is the smallest fixture
//     for the pipelined capability surface.
//   - No further partitioning of `seeds` or `result`. Whole-symbol
//     transfer is the simplest cross-worker dataflow that this
//     example needs.

schedule for "../prog.algo.nuc" {
    workers = { host, producer, consumer };

    // I/O on host (owns the file system).
    place load_input  on host;
    place save_output on host;

    // Stage 1 on producer, stage 2 on consumer.
    place produce   on producer;
    place transform on consumer;

    // Sample n on consumer while sample n+1 is on producer — four
    // samples in flight at steady state.
    loop n : pipeline=4;

    // The inter-stage edge. async + buffer=4 + notify=event is the M4
    // capability set; only pthreads-async satisfies it.
    transfer stream : async, buffer=4, notify=event;

    // Fan-in / fan-out hops to/from host. `load_input(seeds)` and
    // `save_output(result)` both run OUTSIDE the pipelined loop, so
    // the producer-/consumer-side Push/Wait pairs hoist to per-loop
    // granularity. Sync is correct here (TASK-0134 link-step gate
    // requires the consumer to ALSO sit inside the pipelined loop
    // for the buffered async surface to apply).
    transfer seeds  : sync;
    transfer result : sync;
}
